# T2: WiFi hotplug (docs/stock-reconciliation.md §3c "top follow-up").
#
# Stock ships this same path. It is an addon.tar entry -- docs/verification/
# stock-reconciliation/addon-report.txt:25 lists it among addon.tar's 56
# files (marked "ABSENT" there, meaning absent from OUR image at the time
# that report was generated -- this file is what changes that), and the tar's
# provenance (sha256 38e420ce...6fbde) is recorded in
# docs/verification/stock-reconciliation/SOURCE.txt. addon.tar itself is NOT
# unpacked anywhere under work/, so the bytes quoted below were read from the
# extracted stock rootfs instead:
# work/imgroot/etc/udev/rules.d/70-persistent-net.rules (450 bytes, mode 0666).
#
# That file is NOT just two rules. It opens with a five-line banner:
#
#   # This file was automatically generated by the /usr/lib/udev/write_net_rules
#   # program, run by the persistent-net-generator.rules rules file.
#   #
#   # You can modify it, as long as you keep each rule on a single
#   # line, and change only the value of the NAME= key.
#
# which is itself provenance worth recording: write_net_rules emits
# MAC-address-matched rules, not KERNEL=="wlan*" + NAME="wlan0" + RUN+=, so
# stock's copy is a generated file that was then hand-edited. Its two active
# rules are (reproduced verbatim, including stock's single-space spacing):
#
#   SUBSYSTEM=="net", ACTION=="add", DRIVERS=="?*", KERNEL=="wlan*", NAME="wlan0", RUN+="/sbin/ifup -a"
#   SUBSYSTEM=="net", ACTION=="remove", DRIVERS=="?*", KERNEL=="wlan*", RUN+="/sbin/ifdown %k"
#
# Our boot path already covers wlan0/wlan1 (S40network -> ifup -a, and the v9
# pre-up wait loop in etc/network/interfaces for drivers that register
# asynchronously) -- what was missing is a dongle plugged in AFTER boot. This
# file is the hotplug half. Two things below are DELIBERATELY not a verbatim
# copy of stock; both were checked against this image's actual eudev/kernel,
# not assumed.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1) NAME="wlan0" is dropped.
#
# Not because eudev can't do it -- it still can. eudev 3.2.14 implements
# NAME=-driven netif rename via SIOCSIFNAME, and the code path that actually
# compiles in THIS image is:
#
#   output/build/eudev-3.2.14/src/udev/udev-event.c:1003-1012  (the #else arm)
#     -> rename_netif()                                        (:883-885)
#     -> rename_netif_dev_fromname_toname()                    (:813-881)
#     -> ioctl(sk, SIOCSIFNAME, &ifr)                          (:827)
#
# (No claim is made here about what systemd-udev does or when eudev forked
# from it: no systemd source exists anywhere under work/ or output/build/, so
# neither is checkable from this tree. What is checkable is the above, and
# that is the only part this decision rests on.)
#
# A rule in this file (70-) runs before eudev's own default
# /lib/udev/rules.d/80-net-name-slot.rules, which explicitly backs off if a
# NAME has already been assigned (`NAME!="", GOTO="net_name_slot_end"`,
# output/target/lib/udev/rules.d/80-net-name-slot.rules:5) -- so NAME="wlan0"
# here WOULD have taken effect. The reasons not to use it are about
# correctness for OUR image, not a capability gap:
#
#   a) It contradicts this image's two-adapter support -- though in THIS
#      build the damage is bounded, and the honest version of that is
#      narrower than it first looks. board/mister/de10nano/rootfs-overlay/
#      etc/network/interfaces has defined BOTH `auto wlan0` and `auto wlan1`
#      stanzas since the very first rootfs-overlay commit (c1965694, P2.3) --
#      i.e. this image already supports two concurrent WiFi adapters, named
#      by the kernel's own sequential wlan%d numbering. Stock's rule asserts
#      the opposite: that every `wlan*` device is THE one adapter and belongs
#      at the single literal name "wlan0".
#
#      What that actually does here, traced rather than assumed: with a
#      second adapter already holding wlan0, SIOCSIFNAME on the wlan1 device
#      fails with EEXIST; rename_netif_dev_fromname_toname logs "Error
#      changing net interface name" (udev-event.c:874-875) and returns < 0;
#      udev_event_execute_rules logs one "could not rename interface ..."
#      warning (:1008-1010) and SKIPS udev_device_rename; the device keeps
#      its kernel name wlan1, which still matches the `auto wlan1` stanza. So
#      it is a per-event failed-rename warning for zero benefit, not a
#      renaming catastrophe -- and reason (b) is the load-bearing one.
#
#      It is NOT harmless in the general case, though, and that is worth
#      recording because it is one Kconfig symbol away: eudev's
#      collision-avoidance machinery (the "rename to rename_%s / to
#      <base><128-ifindex> and retry for 90s" path at udev-event.c:829-873
#      and :948-1001) really would produce a name matching NEITHER stanza.
#      It is compiled OUT of this image -- output/build/eudev-3.2.14/
#      config.h:5 is `/* #undef ENABLE_RULE_GENERATOR */`, because
#      output/.config:1447 has `# BR2_PACKAGE_EUDEV_RULES_GEN is not set` and
#      work/buildroot/package/eudev/eudev.mk:40-44 therefore passes
#      --disable-rule-generator. Anyone who ever enables that symbol turns
#      stock's NAME= from "noisy no-op" into a real two-adapter regression.
#      Not renaming at all is correct under both settings.
#
#   b) It is unnecessary on this board's actual USB topology. eudev's net_id
#      builtin (output/build/eudev-3.2.14/src/udev/udev-builtin-net_id.c)
#      only ever populates ID_NET_NAME_ONBOARD/_SLOT/_PATH -- the only
#      properties /lib/udev/rules.d/80-net-name-slot.rules consults for a
#      rename -- via names_pci(), and names_pci() requires a PCI ancestor
#      anywhere in the device's parent chain (net_id.c:276-294,
#      `udev_device_get_parent_with_subsystem_devtype(dev, "pci", NULL)`;
#      falls straight to `goto out` with nothing set if none exists). The
#      DE10-Nano's two USB controllers are memory-mapped platform devices,
#      not PCI: output/build/linux-6.18.40/arch/arm/boot/dts/intel/socfpga/
#      socfpga.dtsi:940 `usb0: usb@ffb00000 { compatible = "snps,dwc2"; }`
#      and :953 `usb1: usb@ffb40000 { compatible = "snps,dwc2"; }` -- no PCI
#      bus exists on this SoC at all. So a USB WiFi dongle here NEVER gets a
#      predictable-name property in the first place, and 80-net-name-slot.rules
#      is a permanent no-op for it regardless of what this file does. This
#      matches the hardware-verified behaviour recorded in commit 4cf2fc7
#      ("RTL8822BU auto-connects WPA3 5GHz at boot" via the plain wlan0
#      stanza, no rename involved) and Scripts_MiSTer's other_authors/wifi.sh
#      (the script docs/wifi-parity.md §2 reconciles us against), which
#      hardcodes `ifup wlan0` / `ip link set wlan0 up` (docs/wifi-parity.md:
#      119-120) -- both already assume the kernel-assigned name is the one
#      that sticks.
#
# The kernel's own cfg80211 netdev numbering (wlan0, wlan1, ... in
# registration order) is therefore both the ONLY name that is ever assigned
# on this board and the name the rest of this image (etc/network/interfaces,
# wifi.sh) already hardcodes. Reproducing stock's NAME= would only have added
# failed-rename warnings today, and a two-adapter regression the day
# BR2_PACKAGE_EUDEV_RULES_GEN is ever turned on, for zero naming benefit.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2) RUN+= goes through etc/wifi-hotplug.sh instead of running ifup/ifdown
#    directly, and the add rule targets the specific interface (%k) instead
#    of stock's `-a`.
#
# When this rule actually runs (verified against S10udevd, not assumed): udev
# coldplugs every device present at boot -- package/eudev/S10udevd:42,
# `udevadm trigger --type=devices --action=add` (line :41 is the
# --type=subsystems trigger; :42 is the devices one) -- so wlan0/wlan1's
# "add" uevent fires during ordinary startup too, not just on post-boot
# insertion. It does NOT run in parallel with S40network, though: S10udevd:43
# then runs `udevadm settle --timeout=$SETTLE_TIMEOUT` (SETTLE_TIMEOUT=30,
# S10udevd:22), which blocks rcS until udev's event queue drains. So this
# rule runs to completion inside S10udevd, several init scripts BEFORE
# S40network starts. What races S40network's `ifup -a` is the *detached*
# ifup this rule leaves behind (see below).
#
# That settle is also the strongest reason to detach at all, and it is a
# boot-time regression avoided, not hygiene: a non-detached RUN+= would hold
# the udev event open for the whole bring-up -- the pre-up loop alone polls
# `iw dev $IFACE info` for up to 20s (etc/network/interfaces:18,26), plus the
# stanza's own `post_up sleep 2` -- and `udevadm settle` waits on exactly
# that event. Every boot with WiFi hardware present would stall rcS for
# >20s, and any event chain exceeding the 30s timeout makes settle give up
# and print "udevadm settle failed" before continuing. Detaching keeps the
# udev event short and moves the wait off the boot path entirely.
#
# (Secondary, and the reason the detach is an improvement rather than a
# rescue: eudev's own default event timeout is 180s --
# output/build/eudev-3.2.14/src/udev/udevd.c:72,
# `arg_event_timeout_usec = 180 * USEC_PER_SEC`; nothing in etc/udev/udev.conf
# or package/eudev/S10udevd overrides it -- so a 20s wait would NOT get this
# RUN+= command killed. The problem is settle and worker occupancy, not the
# timeout. Worker occupancy also stacks: a hub with two dongles fires two
# concurrent "add" events. See etc/wifi-hotplug.sh for how the detach is
# actually done and why each piece of it is needed.)
#
# Re-entrancy (verified safe): the detached `ifup <iface>` this rule starts
# and S40network's later boot-time `ifup -a` WILL overlap on every normal
# boot with WiFi hardware present, not just in some rare post-boot-insertion
# edge case. This is safe because ifupdown itself serializes and
# de-duplicates: lock_interface() (output/build/ifupdown-0.8.44/main.c:
# 189-230) takes an exclusive POSIX record lock -- `struct flock lock =
# {.l_type = F_WRLCK, ...}` at main.c:203, `fcntl(fd, F_SETLK, &lock)` at
# :205 with an `fcntl(fd, F_SETLKW, &lock)` blocking fallback at :208, NOT
# flock(2); grep the whole ifupdown-0.8.44 tree and there is no flock() call
# in it -- on a per-interface state file under RUN_DIR ("/run/network/",
# header.h:100 -- tmpfs per etc/fstab, so it is writable even while "/"
# itself is still read-only at boot, ADR 0011's mechanism), and only reads
# the recorded state AFTER acquiring the lock (main.c:217-226). The lock is
# held for the whole up-sequence and released only at function exit
# (main.c:1439-1440). A second `ifup` for the same interface blocks on
# F_SETLKW, then -- once unblocked -- sees the state the first invocation
# already wrote (main.c:1206) and takes the no-op path ("interface %s already
# configured", main.c:1140-1148) instead of launching a second
# wpa_supplicant. Whichever of the two races wins, exactly one
# wpa_supplicant ends up running.
#
# Targeted %k instead of `-a` (deliberate divergence from stock): etc/
# network/interfaces' wlan0/wlan1 stanzas each run the 20s pre-up loop
# described above. `ifup -a` brings up every `auto` stanza, so plugging in
# ONE dongle (say wlan0) would also run `ifup wlan1` if wlan1 is configured
# but has no device -- 20 wasted seconds waiting for a device that will never
# appear, on every single-dongle hotplug. Naming the actual interface (%k)
# avoids that entirely; it is exactly the interface whose "add" uevent just
# fired, which -- per point (1) above -- is also its permanent name.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Kept identical to stock: SUBSYSTEM=="net", the DRIVERS=="?*" guard (only
# fire once the device chain actually has a bound driver), KERNEL=="wlan*",
# and the remove rule's use of %k (ifdown never had stock's NAME= problem --
# it always addressed the interface by its current kernel name).
#
# Verified present at these exact paths in this image (not assumed):
# output/target/usr/sbin/ifup, a 72536-byte ARM ELF; output/target/usr/sbin/
# ifdown -> /usr/sbin/ifup (same binary, dispatches on argv[0]); and
# output/target/sbin -> usr/sbin, so stock's /sbin/ifup and /sbin/ifdown
# spellings resolve to those same two entries (BR2_ROOTFS_MERGED_USR=y).
# output/target/usr/sbin/iw, a 264720-byte ARM ELF, from
# BR2_PACKAGE_IW=y at configs/mister_de10nano_defconfig:698 -- the pre-up
# loop's `iw dev` would be a permanent 20s no-op without it.

SUBSYSTEM=="net", ACTION=="add",    DRIVERS=="?*", KERNEL=="wlan*", RUN+="/etc/wifi-hotplug.sh up %k"
SUBSYSTEM=="net", ACTION=="remove", DRIVERS=="?*", KERNEL=="wlan*", RUN+="/etc/wifi-hotplug.sh down %k"
